ests he's indestructible when we've  learned that he's far from  indestructible one injury at a time so I  think that's the impediment I've been  poking around this idea of what teams  are doing to protect themselves against  the possibility that won't be a season  and they're all operating under the  impression that there's gonna be  something and I've yet to get the sense  that anyone is deliberately restricting  paying out money due to the possibility  that that there won't be a season in  fact I thought most teams wouldn't sign  their rookie draft picks some of the  people I've talked to their attitude is  hey we're going forward we're gonna  treat it like any other year now the  owner may say otherwise  but I don't think that's an impediment I  think the impediment is two things one  making sure he's healthy and 2 what do  you do with him he's gonna want a lot of  money you're gonna have to make him the  starter which team out there at this  point could say to Cam Newton here  you're the guy I mean the Chargers were  one but they have Justin Herbert so  they're off the list  you know the Patriots it's never felt  like a match there Scott Pioli the  former VP of player personnel in New  England said several weeks ago he  doesn't see Bill Belichick and Cam  Newton meshing so I think for cam he's  just got to sit and wait for somebody to  get injured whether it's been rather  hundred  it's Berg again somebody gets injured  and then there's a demand for Cam Newton  for Andy Dalton he's gonna find a spot  and uh yeah and now look you're not  missing anything now cuz there's no  offseason program you're missing zoom  conferences that's it but I just don't I  don't think that that the money is the  issue from the perspective of we may not  have a season I'm not saying it  shouldn't be but I have yet to get the  indication that it is ok so then  follow-up question what  why did Jameis sign for so little and I  know he was saying I know there were  reports that he had bigger offers but it  seems like to me the Saints at that  price when you see the price you know  written out you're like okay the Saint  said if we don't have a season who cares  this is such a little amount of money  for a guy who was you know the number  one pick five years ago and yeah Jameis  has a lot of detractors and rightfully  so with 30 interceptions last year but  if we go back to the conversation we  just had about a quarterback being able  to keep the boat afloat if your starter  goes down  I think Jameis has a higher ceiling in  that respect than a lot of team's  backups and any team that's competing  for a Super Bowl next year would want a  backup like that versus a backup who  might not be able to keep a team afloat  if the starter does go down so that that  whole thing made no sense to me that he  got paid so little and it besides the  fact that an owner saying OK for 1  million yeah we'll do it what let's just  do it well I think that there is a  not-so-subtle psychology to dealing with  Jameis Winston and the Saints waited for  the right time to swoop in and get this  deal done post draft no takers for  Jameis Winston no starting jobs and he  went through that period and I think he  had to work through that period where  once for agency starts and no one comes  after him and he's waiting and he's  waiting and at some point he has to come  to the conclusion on his own it's over  for me right now from the standpoint of  a starter I need to find a place where I  can rebuild things and the obvious  landing spot the Saints when you  consider what happened with Teddy  Bridgewater two years there next thing  you know he's got a three years 66  million dollar contract in Carolina  can't Jameis Winston's made forty five  million in five years with the  Buccaneers 60  six over three is a great outcome if he  can get it and going there and learning  offense from Sean Payton and Drew Brees  it was the perfect time you could argue  maybe he'd be better off lon